docs/progress/logs\20260624-225659_execute-P002.log:678:docs\progress\logs\20260624-221203_execute-P001.log:2878:I’m trying to figure out if I should adjust the drag from the text offset before the emoji to after the emoji. I expect the range to be [1, 2], but I see that the actual range is [1, 3]. This is because the TextPainter is returning an offset of 3 and a text length of 5. So, it seems like DocumentSelection end 3 is including the emoji and the letter B, which is tricky!
docs/progress/logs\20260624-225659_execute-P002.log:679:docs\progress\logs\20260624-221203_execute-P001.log:2960:I need to clarify how the table cell's inline content interacts with pointer dragging or tapping. I'm considering whether Shift and Ctrl+A could help, and it seems I need to document certain behaviors, especially how display length interacts with emojis and mentions. There's a possibility of discrepancies in actual offsets related to emoji representation. I might need to explore using TextPainter for its core mapping, but testing in Flutter will be necessary. I’ll run a command to check for any issues with emojis on Windows.
docs/progress/logs\20260624-225659_execute-P002.log:680:docs\progress\logs\20260624-221203_execute-P001.log:2963:I could use Unicode escapes to sidestep any encoding issues. Using characters like '😄' should work well, and I might want to define a display string constant like '\u{1F604}'. I also need a helper function for global text offsets. It seems important to ensure that the table cells can handle `RichText` properly and convert it with `toPlainText` for the `InlineEmbed` from `TextSpan`, ensuring there are no duplicates. Overall, focusing on RichText in table cells seems beneficial.
